3. Too Much Terraform Being Initialized
   Even with local modules, terraform init still has to walk and wire the entire module tree — resolving references, copying modules into .terraform/modules, and building the dependency graph. If your root module pulls in many nested local modules, this can be surprisingly slow.

Option A — Vendor your modules ahead of time

Run this locally or in a pre-step:

bash
terraform init -get=true
Then commit the .terraform/modules directory. In your lint step, pass:

bash
terraform init -get=false
This skips module resolution entirely since they're already on disk.

Option B — Split your root modules

Instead of one giant root module that references 20 child modules, break it into smaller independent roots:

infrastructure/
networking/ # lint this separately
compute/ # lint this separately
database/ # lint this separately
Each lint run is now scoped and faster.

Option C — Use -backend=false

If you don't need state during lint, skip backend initialization entirely:

bash
terraform init -backend=false
This is faster and avoids any backend connectivity overhead even for local runs.

