5. Module Vendoring as a Pre-Step in Harness
   The core idea: don't let your lint step do the module resolution work. Do it once, cache it, and let lint just use what's already there.

Without vendoring, every time Harness runs your lint stage this happens:

terraform init
→ reads main.tf
→ finds module "rds_alerts" source = "../../../../modules/new_relic/alerts/rds_alerts"
→ walks that module's dependencies
→ walks THOSE dependencies
→ copies everything into .terraform/modules/
→ THEN lint can start
For your repo that chain gets long fast, and it happens fresh every single pipeline run.

Option A — Vendor via a Harness Run Step (Pre-Step)
Add a Run step before your Lint step in the same stage:

yaml
steps:

- step:
  type: Run
  name: Vendor Terraform Modules
  identifier: vendor_modules
  spec:
  shell: Sh
  command: |
  cd stacks/team_a/rds/dev
  terraform init \
   -backend=false \
   -get=true \
   No-color
  envVariables:
  TF_PLUGIN_CACHE_DIR: /harness/terraform-plugin-cache

- step:
  type: TerraformPlan # or your lint step type
  name: Terraform Lint
  identifier: terraform_lint
  spec:
  configuration:
  configFiles:
  store:
  spec:
  folderPath: stacks/team_a/rds/dev
  commandFlags: - commandType: Init
  flag: "-get=false" # <-- skip resolution, use what's already there
  The -get=false flag on the lint init is the key — it tells Terraform "don't go find modules, they're already on disk."

Option B — Commit .terraform/modules to the Repo
Run locally:

bash
cd stacks/team_a/rds/dev
terraform init -backend=false
Then commit the result:

bash
git add .terraform/modules
git commit -m "vendor modules for team_a rds stack"
And add this to your .gitignore to be explicit about what you ARE committing:

gitignore

# Ignore everything in .terraform EXCEPT vendored modules

.terraform/\*
!.terraform/modules
Now Harness never has to resolve modules at all — they're already in the repo. Your lint step just runs against what's checked in.

Tradeoff: Your repo gets bigger, and you have to re-vendor when modules change. For a modules repo like yours that's actively being developed right now, this can get noisy. Better suited once your modules stabilize.

Option C — Shared Plugin/Module Cache on the Delegate
This is the most scalable option for when you have multiple teams and stacks running concurrently. You set up a persistent cache directory on the delegate so module resolution is shared across all pipeline runs:

yaml

# In your delegate deployment / values.yaml

env:

- name: TF_PLUGIN_CACHE_DIR
  value: /opt/harness/terraform-cache/plugins
- name: TF_DATA_DIR
  value: /opt/harness/terraform-cache/modules
  Then mount a persistent volume to that path on the delegate pod:

yaml
volumeMounts:

- name: terraform-cache
  mountPath: /opt/harness/terraform-cache

volumes:

- name: terraform-cache
  persistentVolumeClaim:
  claimName: terraform-cache-pvc
  Now when team_a/rds/dev runs and resolves modules/new_relic/alerts/rds_alerts, that result sits in the cache. When team_b/rds/dev runs next and needs the same module — it's already there. No re-resolution.

This is the option that scales with you as you add teams, because every new team stack benefits from modules already cached by previous runs.

Which Option for Your Situation
Option Best For Tradeoff
A — Run pre-step Right now, quick win Re-vendors every run
B — Commit to repo Stable/frozen modules Repo bloat, manual re-vendor on changes
C — Delegate cache Multi-team scale Requires PVC setup on delegate
Given where you are — actively building modules, one team now but more coming — I'd do Option A immediately to unblock yourself, and set up Option C on the delegate before you onboard the next team.

